{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His early life is not well documented. He succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at the age of 12. His mother Hodierna acted as regent for him until he came of age.\n\n*Marriage and Alliance*\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174. The marriage was arranged by King Amalric I of Jerusalem to strengthen the alliance between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. Eschiva was a member of the powerful Bures family and brought a significant dowry to the marriage.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Imprisonment*\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He led the vanguard of the Crusader army but was defeated by Saladin's forces. Raymond III was captured by Saladin and imprisoned in the citadel of Nablus. He was later released and exiled to Tripoli.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1187 at the age of 47. He had no children with his wife Eschiva. The County of Tripoli passed to his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. Raymond III was remembered as a brave and chivalrous knight who fought against the Muslim forces in the Holy Land.\n\n*Legacy*\nRaymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a member of the powerful House of Toulouse and played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was also a skilled military leader who fought against the Muslim forces in the Holy Land. Despite his bravery and military prowess Raymond III's reign was marked by defeat and imprisonment.\n\nRaymond III's life and reign are a testament to the complexities and challenges faced by the Crusade